.Dd September 22, 2026
.Dt PW 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pw
.Nd create, remove, modify and display users and groups
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl q
.Ar command
.Op Ar name Ns | Ns Ar id
.Op Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
takes the same command line as FreeBSD's
.Nm :
.Cm useradd , userdel , usermod , usershow , usernext , lock , unlock ,
.Cm groupadd , groupdel , groupmod , groupshow
and
.Cm groupnext ,
in their
.Cm user add
and
.Cm adduser
spellings too.
What differs on NextBSD is where an account goes.
.Ss Where accounts live
.Bl -bullet
.It
Root, the system users and every group in
.Pa /etc/group
stay in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
and
.Pa /etc/group .
For them FreeBSD's
.Nm ,
kept at
.Pa /usr/libexec/bsd/pw ,
is run with exactly the arguments given, so a package install script's
.Dl pw useradd -n _svc -u 123 -s /usr/sbin/nologin -d /nonexistent
lands where it always has.
.It
Proper users and their groups are records in
.Pa Users.plist
and
.Pa Groups.plist
under
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices ,
the same files
.Xr dscli 8
edits and the name service reads.
.El
.Pp
An existing account is found where it is.
A new user is a system account when its uid
.Pq Fl u
is 499 or below, or its shell is
.Pa /usr/sbin/nologin
with no home
.Pq Fl d Pa /nonexistent No or none ;
otherwise it is a directory user.
A new group is a system group when its gid
.Pq Fl g
is 499 or below, or it has no gid and a name starting with
.Ql _ ;
otherwise it is a directory group.
.Ss Directory accounts
For a directory user
.Nm
honours
.Fl n , u , c , d , g , G , m , s , o , w , h , H
and
.Fl l ,
with these differences from FreeBSD:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The home is always
.Pa /Local/Users/ Ns Ar name ;
.Fl d
must name it and
.Fl m
creates it with the skeleton from
.Pa /usr/share/skel .
.It
.Fl c
sets the record's
.Va realName .
The default shell is
.Pa /bin/zsh .
.It
.Fl w
accepts
.Cm no
.Pq no usable password, the default ,
.Cm none
.Po
log in without one: the record's
.Va noPassword
.Pc
and
.Cm yes
.Pq the user name .
.Fl h
reads the password from a file descriptor and
.Fl H
a pre-encrypted SHA-512 hash.
.It
.Fl G
lists directory groups only; a directory user cannot be put into a group
from
.Pa /etc/group .
.It
Login classes, expiry dates and the other FreeBSD-only fields
.Pq Fl L , e , p , i , b
are refused.
.El
.Pp
.Cm usershow -a
and
.Cm groupshow -a
print the directory records and then FreeBSD's.
.Cm lock
and
.Cm unlock
prefix the hash with
.Ql *LOCKED* ,
as FreeBSD does.
.Pp
On a joined client the accounts in use are the server's and
.Nm
refuses to edit them.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
directory users
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist
directory groups
.It Pa /etc/master.passwd , /etc/group
root, the system users and groups
.It Pa /usr/libexec/bsd/pw
FreeBSD's
.Nm
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chpass 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr dscli 8 ,
.Xr rmuser 8 ,
.Xr vipw 8
